# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tflush tag[2] oldtag[2]
size[4] Rflush tag[2]
```

# DESCRIPTION

When the reply to a request is no longer needed, as when a process reading is interrupted, the client sends `Tflush`, type 108, naming the request's tag in <oldtag>.
The server answers `Rflush`, type 109, with the `Tflush`'s own <tag>, not <oldtag>.

In 9P2000 the server answers at once: if <oldtag> is a request still pending, it abandons it and sends no reply for it; either way it answers `Rflush`, and never `Rerror`.
A reply that arrives before the `Rflush` is honoured, since its request may have changed something, such as a fid made by a walk; a request with no reply before the `Rflush` is treated as never sent.
The client does not use <oldtag> again until the `Rflush` has come.
Several flushes of one request are answered in order, and a flush of a tag no request has, or of a request already answered, is answered all the same.

A VectraOS server answers every `Tflush` at once with `Rflush`, whatever <oldtag> names.
It completes each request as it arrives, so nothing is pending when the `Tflush` is read, except a `Tread`, `Twrite` or `Topen` the file server cannot do yet, such as a read of a console with nothing typed or an open of a listen file with no call.
On a ring such a request is held while the connection takes more (rings(7)), and a `Tflush` of it drops it, unanswered, before the `Rflush` goes out (M6 step 6d4a); a request a thread is serving, the server let go meanwhile (`p9_release`, M6 step 6d5a), is answered first, and the `Rflush` after it.

VectraOS's ring client sends `Tflush` for a call whose wait is interrupted, if the caller wants to hear of the interruption, and for a call past its connection's timeout.
It keeps the call's tag until the `Rflush`, and a reply that comes first is the call's answer; otherwise the call returns `VX_ERR_INTERRUPTED` or `VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT`.
No `Rflush` within the timeout (5 seconds without one) ends the connection.
Over TCP the client sends one request at a time and no `Tflush`.

